MECHANICAL SERVICES MASTER SERVICE AGREEMENT

THIS MECHANICAL SERVICES MASTER SERVICE AGREEMENT (this "Agreement") is made and entered into effective as of the 1st day of July, 2021 (the "Effective Date"), by and between SUNPOINT PUBLIC SCHOOLS COOPERATIVE, an Illinois intergovernmental cooperative consisting of eleven (11) school districts with its principal office located at 800 South Plum Grove Road, Palatine, Illinois 60067 ("Cooperative"), and MERIDIAN MECHANICAL OF ILLINOIS, LLC, an Illinois limited liability company with its principal place of business located at 1455 Busse Road, Elk Grove Village, Illinois 60007 ("Contractor").

RECITALS:

WHEREAS, the Cooperative issued that certain Request for Proposal No. SPSC-2021-07 (the "RFP") soliciting qualified contractors to provide mechanical services, including heating, ventilation, air conditioning (HVAC), boiler, and plumbing maintenance and repair services at the facilities of the Cooperative's member school districts; and

WHEREAS, the Cooperative is an intergovernmental cooperative formed under the Intergovernmental Cooperation Act, 5 ILCS 220/1 et seq., and serves as a purchasing and operational entity for eleven school districts: Community Unit School District 15, Community Unit School District 26, Elementary District 23, Elementary District 26, High School District 211, High School District 214, Township High School District 225, Barrington Community Unit School District 220, Palatine Community Consolidated School District 15, Libertyville Elementary School District 70, and Grayslake Community Unit School District 46;

WHEREAS, the Cooperative desires to enter into this Master Service Agreement with Contractor to establish the terms and conditions under which Contractor will provide mechanical services on an as-needed basis, with specific work to be authorized through individually executed Task Orders;

WHEREAS, Contractor has demonstrated the qualifications, experience, insurance coverage, bonding capacity, and compliance certifications to perform such services in a professional, timely, and workmanlike manner, and in full compliance with the Illinois Prevailing Wage Act, 820 ILCS 130/0.01 et seq.; and

NOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements contained herein and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, the parties agree as follows:

1. SCOPE OF WORK

1.1 General Scope. Contractor shall provide comprehensive mechanical services to the Cooperative's member school districts, including but not limited to the following categories:

(a) HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning) maintenance, including filter changes, inspections, seasonal preparation, testing, and calibration of systems;

(b) HVAC repair services, including diagnosis and correction of malfunctions, replacement of components, and restoration of system operation;

(c) HVAC emergency services, including twenty-four-hour response to heating or cooling failures that impact occupant comfort or safety;

(d) Boiler maintenance, including inspections, cleaning, testing, and preventive maintenance of heating systems;

(e) Boiler repair services, including diagnosis, component replacement, and system restoration;

(f) Boiler emergency services for loss of heat or boiler malfunction;

(g) Plumbing maintenance, including inspections, cleaning, and testing of water supply, drainage, and waste systems;

(h) Plumbing repair services, including diagnosis and correction of leaks, clogs, fixture malfunctions, and drainage problems;

(i) Plumbing installation services for fixtures, components, and system upgrades within the scope of repair and maintenance;

(j) Plumbing emergency services for water supply interruptions, backups, or fixture failures;

(k) Related mechanical and trade work as may be required to support the HVAC, boiler, and plumbing systems at member district facilities, including coordination with other trades and provision of labor for installation work.

1.2 Standards of Performance. All work shall be performed in a professional, workmanlike manner in accordance with:

(a) Applicable federal and state laws and regulations, including the Illinois Building Code, Illinois Plumbing Code, Illinois Mechanical Code, and all applicable electrical codes;

(b) All local building codes, plumbing codes, and mechanical codes in effect in the municipalities where the work is performed;

(c) Manufacturer's specifications and recommendations for all equipment and materials;

(d) The current standards of the mechanical trades as established by industry organizations, including the American Society of Heating, Refrigerating and Air-Conditioning Engineers (ASHRAE), the Plumbing-Heating-Cooling Contractors Association, and similar industry bodies;

(e) All applicable occupational safety and health standards, including OSHA requirements.

1.3 Availability and Response Times. Contractor shall maintain appropriate staffing, equipment, and service facilities to meet the following response requirements:

(a) For routine maintenance requests authorized by Task Order, Contractor shall schedule and commence work within five (5) business days of receipt of the Task Order;

(b) For emergency service requests, Contractor shall respond and dispatch qualified personnel to the requesting facility within four (4) hours of initial contact during business hours (Monday through Friday, 7:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m.) or within six (6) hours for requests made outside business hours, provided that the member district has contacted Contractor's designated emergency number;

(c) Specific response times and availability requirements may be modified for particular facilities or task orders by written agreement of the parties.

1.4 Member Districts Served. The Cooperative's member school districts for whom services may be requested under this Agreement are the following eleven (11) school districts:

(i) Community Unit School District 15 (Palatine)
(ii) Community Unit School District 26 (Arlington Heights)
(iii) Elementary District 23 (Elk Grove)
(iv) Elementary District 26 (Prospect Heights)
(v) High School District 211 (Elk Grove)
(vi) High School District 214 (Arlington Heights)
(vii) Township High School District 225 (Mount Prospect)
(viii) Barrington Community Unit School District 220
(ix) Palatine Community Consolidated School District 15
(x) Libertyville Elementary School District 70
(xi) Grayslake Community Unit School District 46

Each member district is an authorized user of this Agreement and may request services on behalf of its facilities.

1.5 Excluded Services. Notwithstanding the general scope set forth above, Contractor shall have no obligation to provide the following services unless expressly authorized by a written Task Order that clearly identifies such services as being within the scope of work:

(a) Major system replacements, equipment replacement, or capital improvement projects extending beyond the scope of repair and maintenance;

(b) Design, engineering, or architectural services;

(c) Asbestos abatement, lead paint removal, or other hazardous materials work;

(d) Removal or disposal of regulated medical waste or biohazardous materials;

(e) Services requiring specialized certifications or licenses not held by Contractor or its employees;

(f) Work in facilities or areas requiring specialized security clearances or background investigations not held by Contractor;

(g) Structural repairs or reinforcement;

(h) Any work not expressly authorized and described in a fully executed Task Order.

1.6 Coordination with Other Contractors. When multiple contractors are working on a member district's facilities, Contractor shall coordinate its work schedule and procedures with other contractors and the member district to minimize disruption to facility operations and ensure safety. Contractor shall not be responsible for coordination of work by other contractors, and disputes regarding scheduling or coordination shall be resolved by the member district.

2. TASK ORDERS

2.1 Authorization Requirement and Issuance. No work shall be commenced by Contractor unless and until Contractor has received a written Task Order from an authorized representative of the Cooperative or one of its member districts. Each Task Order shall be issued on a form substantially similar to the Task Order form attached as Exhibit A, or in such other form as may be agreed upon in writing by the parties and the requesting member district.

2.2 Required Contents of Task Orders. Each Task Order shall include, at a minimum, the following information:

(a) A clear and detailed description of the work to be performed, including the system(s) affected, nature of the repair or maintenance, specific equipment or areas involved, and any special procedures or precautions;

(b) The name, address, contact telephone number, and email address of the requesting member district and the authorized contact person or facility manager;

(c) The location of the work, including building name and address, room or area designation if applicable, and access instructions;

(d) The estimated value or budget authorization for the Task Order;

(e) Any special requirements, restrictions, specifications, safety considerations, or environmental protocols applicable to the particular project;

(f) The required timeline for completion, including any specific start date, completion deadline, or hours during which work must or must not be performed;

(g) The authorization signature, printed name, and title of the person issuing the Task Order;

(h) Any project-specific insurance or bonding requirements in excess of those required under Section 6 of this Agreement;

(i) Whether work is subject to prevailing wage requirements; and

(j) Any other information necessary for Contractor to understand and perform the work as requested.

2.3 Acceptance and Confirmation. Contractor shall indicate its acceptance of each Task Order by executing and returning a signed copy to the requesting member district within two (2) business days of receipt, provided that Contractor's acceptance shall not be unreasonably withheld or delayed. If Contractor cannot accept a Task Order as issued, Contractor shall notify the requesting member district in writing within two (2) business days, stating the specific reasons for non-acceptance and proposing any alternative arrangements or modifications that would permit Contractor to perform the work. Failure to respond to a Task Order within two (2) business days shall constitute acceptance.

2.4 Pricing and Billing. All pricing for work performed under Task Orders shall be in accordance with the Compensation section of this Agreement (Section 3). Contractor shall submit invoices within fifteen (15) business days of substantial completion of each Task Order, with detailed itemization of all labor hours at the applicable prevailing wage rates, all materials and supplies with supporting invoices or receipts, and all reimbursable expenses, along with the applicable markups as set forth in Section 3. Contractor shall include a copy of the Task Order with each invoice to facilitate payment processing.

2.5 Changes and Modifications. No changes to the scope of work, timeline, or pricing under a Task Order shall be binding unless documented in a written change order signed by an authorized representative of the requesting member district and Contractor. Change orders shall be subject to the same compensation provisions as the original Task Order and shall comply with all prevailing wage requirements. Contractor shall not proceed with any changed work until a change order is fully executed.

2.6 Warranty and Inspections. Upon completion of work authorized by a Task Order, Contractor shall notify the requesting member district in writing, and such district shall have the right to inspect the completed work prior to formal acceptance. If deficiencies are identified, Contractor shall remedy such deficiencies at no additional cost to the Cooperative or member district within five (5) business days of receipt of written notice of deficiency, or such other timeline as agreed in writing. Contractor warrants that all work shall comply with applicable law, codes, and standards, and shall be free from defects in workmanship and materials for a period of one (1) year from the date of substantial completion.

3. COMPENSATION

3.1 Pricing Structure and Methodology. Compensation for services performed under this Agreement shall be based on the following pricing model:

(a) Labor. All labor shall be charged at the applicable Illinois Prevailing Wage rates as established by the Illinois Department of Labor for the specific trade, classification, and county in which the work is performed, plus a markup of forty-two percent (42%) to cover Contractor's overhead, administrative costs, burden, benefits, risk, and profit. Prevailing wage rates are updated annually and may vary by trade classification and geographic location. Current rates for Cook County include: Plumber $47.45 to $52.18 per hour depending on experience and classification; HVAC Technician $49.62 to $54.31 per hour; Boiler Technician $48.90 to $53.12 per hour. These rates are subject to annual adjustment as published by the Illinois Department of Labor. Contractor shall apply the prevailing wage rate in effect on the date work is performed, not the rate in effect on the Effective Date. The 42% markup shall be applied to the then-current rate.

(b) Materials and Supplies. All materials, supplies, equipment, and parts purchased for work performed under Task Orders shall be charged at Contractor's documented cost, which shall include the actual price paid to the vendor, plus a markup of fifteen percent (15%) to cover handling, storage, inventory management, waste, and administrative processing costs. Contractor shall provide copies of material invoices or receipts with project invoices to document actual costs. For materials purchased by Contractor's suppliers on Contractor's account or materials obtained through third-party arrangements, Contractor shall document the actual cost incurred.

(c) Travel and Mobilization. Contractor shall charge a travel and mobilization fee of fifty dollars ($50.00) per initial site visit to a member district's facility to cover Contractor's travel time and initial setup. Return visits to the same facility on the same day shall not incur additional travel charges. Mileage shall be reimbursed at the rate established by the Internal Revenue Service in effect at the time of service, for any distance exceeding twenty (20) miles from Contractor's principal place of business at 1455 Busse Road, Elk Grove Village, Illinois 60007.

(d) Emergency Services. Services provided outside of Contractor's normal business hours (8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Monday through Friday, excluding state and federal holidays) shall be considered emergency services and shall be subject to a one hundred percent (100%) premium on labor, applied in addition to the prevailing wage rate and the 42% markup described above. Services performed on weekends and holidays shall be charged at the applicable prevailing wage rate in effect on the date of work performance, plus the 42% markup, plus the 100% emergency premium.

(e) Equipment Rental and Specialized Tools. If specialized equipment or tools not normally maintained by Contractor must be rented or purchased for a specific Task Order, the actual cost of such rental or purchase, plus a reasonable handling markup not to exceed 10%, may be charged as a reimbursable expense, provided that Contractor obtains authorization from the member district prior to incurring such cost.

3.2 Invoicing, Documentation, and Payment Terms.

(a) Invoice Submission. Contractor shall submit invoices within fifteen (15) business days of completion or substantial completion of work under a Task Order. Each invoice shall reference the Task Order number, member district name, and facility location. Invoices shall include:

(i) A copy of the fully executed Task Order;

(ii) Itemized labor records showing the date of work, employee name and trade classification, actual hours worked (not rounded), the applicable hourly prevailing wage rate, and calculation of the 42% markup;

(iii) A list of all materials and supplies utilized, with copies of supporting vendor invoices or receipts showing actual cost, description of materials, and calculation of the 15% markup;

(iv) Documentation of any reimbursable expenses, including travel, mileage, equipment rental, or other costs authorized in the Task Order;

(v) A detailed cost breakdown showing all components and the application of applicable markups; and

(vi) Any supporting documentation necessary for the member district to verify quantities, pricing, and compliance with prevailing wage requirements.

(b) Payment Terms. Payment shall be due within thirty (30) days of receipt of a compliant invoice. Invoices shall be submitted to the business office of the requesting member district at the address specified in the Task Order or as provided by the district. In the event of a dispute regarding an invoice, the quality of work, or an alleged violation of this Agreement, the Cooperative or member district may withhold payment of the disputed amount pending resolution, provided that all undisputed portions of the invoice shall be paid within the 30-day window.

(c) Late Payment. Invoices not paid by the due date shall accrue interest at the rate of one percent (1%) per month or the maximum rate permitted by Illinois law, whichever is less, calculated from the due date until payment is received.

3.3 Material Sourcing and Approved Vendors. 

(a) To the extent practicable and when available, Contractor shall source materials from vendors approved by the Cooperative or member districts. The Cooperative shall maintain a current list of approved vendors for mechanical supplies, HVAC equipment, plumbing products, and related materials, and shall provide such list to Contractor. Contractor shall coordinate material sourcing with member district preferences and prior purchasing relationships.

(b) For single Task Order purchases of materials exceeding five hundred dollars ($500.00) in value, Contractor shall obtain and document at least two competitive quotes from different vendors unless emergency circumstances, specific equipment compatibility issues, or unavailability of alternate products prevent such comparison. Contractor shall provide copies of quotes with the invoice to document that pricing obtained was reasonable and competitive.

(c) Contractor shall not be required to use specific vendors if doing so would materially increase costs or delay completion of emergency work, provided that Contractor documents the reasons for utilizing alternate vendors.

3.4 Prevailing Wage Documentation and Compliance.

(a) Contractor shall maintain certified payroll records for all employees performing work under this Agreement, documenting compliance with prevailing wage requirements. Such records shall include employee name, trade classification, hours worked, date of work, hourly rate paid, and fringe benefits provided.

(b) Records shall be prepared in the format specified by the Illinois Department of Labor and shall be submitted to the Cooperative within five (5) business days of a request, or at such other frequency as may be required by the Cooperative or member districts.

(c) Contractor shall ensure that all employees have been properly classified according to their actual work assignments and prevailing wage requirements, and shall not misclassify workers in lower-paid categories to reduce prevailing wage costs.

