3.6 Volume Discounts and Adjustments.

(a) The Cooperative recognizes that significant volume purchasing opportunities may arise during the contract term as member districts collectively request services.

(b) To the extent that the Cooperative's member districts collectively generate substantial material purchases, services, or equipment needs, the parties may negotiate volume discounts and rebates in writing, to be applied to future invoices or credited to the Cooperative through reduced markups or pricing.

(c) Any volume discounts or pricing adjustments must be documented in a written amendment signed by authorized representatives of both the Cooperative and Contractor.

4. PREVAILING WAGE COMPLIANCE

4.1 Prevailing Wage Requirement and Applicability. This Agreement is subject to the requirements of the Illinois Prevailing Wage Act, 820 ILCS 130/0.01 et seq. (the "Prevailing Wage Act"), which applies to all work performed on public buildings and public works projects in Illinois. All labor performed under this Agreement, including wages, salaries, fringe benefits, or other forms of compensation provided to employees, shall be paid in accordance with the prevailing wage rates established and published by the Illinois Department of Labor for the applicable trade, skill, and classification, and for the geographic location where work is performed (Cook County and other counties as applicable).

4.2 Current Prevailing Wage Rates. The prevailing wage rates applicable as of the Effective Date of this Agreement for Cook County are as follows, and shall be automatically adjusted annually each July 1st in accordance with the Illinois Department of Labor's published determinations:

Plumber (General): $47.45 per hour base wage
Plumber (Licensed): $52.18 per hour base wage
HVAC Technician: $49.62 per hour base wage
HVAC Technician (Senior): $54.31 per hour base wage
Boiler Technician: $48.90 per hour base wage
Boiler Technician (Licensed): $53.12 per hour base wage

Prevailing wage rates are subject to change annually. Contractor shall be solely responsible for obtaining current rates from the Illinois Department of Labor website and shall apply the applicable rates in effect on the date work is performed. The 42% markup shall be applied to the then-current prevailing wage rate for the specific work date, not to historical rates or rates in effect on the Effective Date of this Agreement.

4.3 Fringe Benefits and Contributions. In addition to the prevailing wage rates set forth in Section 4.2, Contractor shall provide all fringe benefits required under the Prevailing Wage Act and as established by the Illinois Department of Labor, which may include:

(a) Contributions to health and welfare funds or health insurance;

(b) Contributions to pension or annuity funds;

(c) Vacation or paid leave funds;

(d) Apprenticeship or training fund contributions;

(e) Such other fringe benefits as established by the State of Illinois for the applicable trade and classification.

Fringe benefit contributions shall be documented separately on invoices and payroll records.

4.4 Subcontractors and Compliance Responsibility. Contractor warrants that:

(a) It shall perform all work either with its own employees or shall engage only subcontractors who are similarly bound by and compliant with the prevailing wage requirements of the Prevailing Wage Act;

(b) Contractor shall be solely responsible for ensuring that all subcontractors comply with prevailing wage requirements and shall include the prevailing wage requirements and references to this Agreement in all subcontracts;

(c) Contractor shall monitor subcontractor compliance and shall not make final payment to any subcontractor until Contractor has verified that all prevailing wages have been paid;

(d) Contractor shall remain liable to the Cooperative, member districts, and affected employees for any prevailing wage violations by subcontractors.

4.5 Compliance Certification. Contractor shall execute and deliver to the Cooperative a certification substantially in the form attached as Exhibit B, stating that Contractor is familiar with the prevailing wage requirements of the Illinois Prevailing Wage Act and that all employees and subcontractors shall be paid in accordance with the prevailing wage rates established by the Illinois Department of Labor. Such certification shall be provided concurrently with execution of this Agreement and annually upon renewal.

4.6 Record Keeping, Audit, and Inspection. 

(a) Contractor shall maintain certified payroll records for all employees performing work under this Agreement for a period of three (3) years following completion of work.

(b) Contractor shall provide such records to the Cooperative, member districts, or the Illinois Department of Labor upon request, and shall cooperate fully with any audit, inspection, or investigation conducted by these entities regarding compliance with prevailing wage requirements.

(c) Contractor shall make payroll records available for inspection at Contractor's principal place of business or at such other location as designated by the Cooperative, during normal business hours and upon reasonable notice.

(d) The Cooperative reserves the right to audit Contractor's prevailing wage compliance at any time during the contract term and for three years thereafter.

4.7 Remedies for Non-Compliance. 

(a) In the event of Contractor's failure to pay prevailing wages as required by this Agreement and the Prevailing Wage Act, Contractor shall be liable to the affected employees for back wages, penalties, interest, and attorney's fees as provided under the Prevailing Wage Act, 820 ILCS 130/0.01 et seq.

(b) The Cooperative and member districts may withhold payment from Contractor in an amount sufficient to secure payment of back wages owed to workers, or may make direct payment to affected workers and deduct such amounts from Contractor's invoices.

(c) Repeated or willful violations of prevailing wage requirements, or failure to maintain required payroll records, shall constitute grounds for immediate termination of this Agreement for cause and shall preclude Contractor from bidding on future work with the Cooperative or its member districts.

(d) Contractor shall indemnify and hold the Cooperative and member districts harmless from any liability arising from Contractor's failure to comply with prevailing wage requirements, including any claims filed by workers or investigations by the Illinois Department of Labor.

5. TERM AND RENEWAL

5.1 Initial Term. This Agreement shall be effective as of July 1, 2021 (the "Effective Date") and shall continue in full force and effect for a period of three (3) years, expiring on June 30, 2024 (the "Initial Term"), unless earlier terminated in accordance with Section 10 of this Agreement. Upon the Effective Date, this Agreement immediately becomes binding upon the parties and Contractor shall be prepared to respond to service requests from member districts.

5.2 Renewal Options. The Cooperative shall have the right, exercisable at its sole discretion, to renew this Agreement for two (2) successive one-year renewal periods, each extending from July 1st through June 30th of the following year, on the same terms and conditions as contained herein, provided that all of the following conditions are satisfied:

(a) Contractor has performed all material obligations under this Agreement in a satisfactory manner, as determined in the sole discretion of the Cooperative;

(b) Contractor has not been subject to material breaches, defaults, or violations of this Agreement that remain uncured;

(c) Contractor remains in full compliance with all insurance and bonding requirements specified in Section 6;

(d) There are no ongoing disputes regarding Contractor's invoices, work quality, the timeliness of performance, or compliance with prevailing wage requirements;

(e) Contractor has maintained all required licenses, certifications, and qualifications throughout the contract term;

(f) The Cooperative provides written notice of its election to renew at least sixty (60) days prior to the expiration of the then-current term.

The Cooperative's determination regarding renewal shall be made in its sole and exclusive discretion and shall not be subject to appeal or challenge by Contractor.

5.3 Exercise of Renewal Options. The Cooperative exercised its right to renew this Agreement for the first one-year renewal period effective July 1, 2022 through June 30, 2023. The Cooperative thereafter exercised its right to renew for the second one-year renewal period effective July 1, 2023 through June 30, 2024. Pursuant to the two successive renewals, this Agreement is currently in effect and shall remain in effect through June 30, 2024. A potential third renewal option may be exercised at the Cooperative's discretion for the period July 1, 2024 through June 30, 2025, subject to satisfaction of the conditions set forth in Section 5.2 above.

5.4 Continuation During Renewal Negotiation. In the event that the parties are actively negotiating terms for renewal or a successor agreement and have not finalized such renewal or successor agreement by the expiration date of the then-current term, Contractor shall, upon the written request of the Cooperative, continue to perform services under the existing terms and conditions of this Agreement on a month-to-month basis until such time as a renewal or successor agreement is fully executed by both parties, or until the Cooperative notifies Contractor in writing that services are to be terminated. Any such continuation shall not constitute a waiver of any terms or conditions of this Agreement, nor shall it modify the status of Contractor as an independent contractor.

5.5 Pricing Adjustments Upon Renewal. Pricing for any renewal term shall remain subject to the pricing structure set forth in Section 3 of this Agreement. Labor shall continue to be charged at the prevailing wage rates in effect at the time work is performed, plus the 42% markup; materials shall continue to be charged at cost plus 15%. Annual adjustments to prevailing wage rates shall be automatically incorporated into invoicing without the necessity of a formal amendment to this Agreement, as published by the Illinois Department of Labor.

6. INSURANCE AND BONDS

6.1 Commercial General Liability Insurance. Contractor shall obtain and maintain, at Contractor's own expense and at all times during the term of this Agreement, a policy of commercial general liability insurance covering bodily injury, property damage, products and completed operations, and personal injury, with minimum limits of one million dollars ($1,000,000) per occurrence and two million dollars ($2,000,000) general aggregate. Such policy shall:

(a) Be issued by insurers rated A-:VII or better in the most current Best's Insurance Guide and authorized to do business in the State of Illinois;

(b) Include the Cooperative and each of its member school districts as additional insureds, with respect to liability arising out of Contractor's work;

(c) Be written on a primary basis and not contributory with any insurance carried by the Cooperative or member districts;

(d) Include a waiver of subrogation in favor of the Cooperative and its member districts;

(e) Provide for written notice to the Cooperative at least thirty (30) days prior to cancellation, non-renewal, or material modification of coverage; and

(f) Include contractual liability coverage for Contractor's indemnity obligations under this Agreement.

6.2 Workers' Compensation Insurance. Contractor shall obtain and maintain workers' compensation insurance as required by the laws of the State of Illinois, with minimum limits of one million dollars ($1,000,000) for employers' liability. Contractor shall either operate as a sole proprietor with no employees or shall maintain such insurance for all employees performing work under this Agreement. Contractor shall provide proof of workers' compensation insurance prior to commencing any work.

6.3 Automobile Liability Insurance. Contractor shall maintain comprehensive automobile liability insurance covering all vehicles used in the performance of work under this Agreement, whether owned, leased, or hired, with minimum combined single limits of one million dollars ($1,000,000) for bodily injury and property damage per occurrence. Such policy shall:

(a) Include the Cooperative as an additional insured;

(b) Provide for written notice to the Cooperative thirty (30) days prior to cancellation or material modification;

(c) Cover all vehicles used to transport workers, equipment, or materials to member district facilities.

6.4 Contractor's Equipment and Tools. Contractor shall be solely responsible for insuring all of its own equipment, tools, materials, vehicles, and other property, and for protecting such property against loss or damage from theft, fire, vandalism, or other casualty. The Cooperative, member districts, and their respective officials and employees shall not be responsible for any loss, theft, or damage to Contractor's property, regardless of how such loss or damage occurs.

6.5 Performance Bond. Contractor shall obtain and maintain a performance bond in the amount of ten percent (10%) of the total estimated annual costs under this Agreement. For the first contract year (2021-2022), estimated annual costs are two hundred fifty thousand dollars ($250,000), resulting in a required performance bond of twenty-five thousand dollars ($25,000). The performance bond shall:

(a) Guarantee Contractor's faithful performance of all obligations under this Agreement;

(b) Be renewed annually prior to each July 1st renewal date;

(c) Name the Cooperative and its member districts as co-obligees;

(d) Be issued by a surety licensed to do business in Illinois and rated A-:VI or better in Best's Insurance Guide;

(e) Provide for thirty (30) days' written notice to the Cooperative prior to cancellation or non-renewal.

6.6 Labor and Materials Payment Bond. Contractor shall obtain and maintain a labor and materials payment bond in the amount equal to the performance bond amount, currently twenty-five thousand dollars ($25,000), to guarantee payment of all workers and suppliers. The payment bond shall:

(a) Guarantee that all workers shall be paid in accordance with prevailing wage requirements;

(b) Guarantee that all material suppliers shall be paid in full for goods and services provided;

(c) Be renewed annually prior to each July 1st renewal date;

(d) Name the Cooperative and its member districts as co-obligees and include language permitting workers and suppliers to bring claims directly against the bond;

(e) Be issued by a surety licensed in Illinois and rated as specified above.

6.7 Proof of Insurance and Bonds. Contractor shall deliver to the Cooperative comprehensive certificates of insurance evidencing all coverage required under this Section 6 prior to commencing any work. Such certificates shall:

(a) Be submitted on the ACORD form or other standard insurance certificate form;

(b) Show policy numbers, effective dates, expiration dates, limits of liability, and required endorsements;

(c) Name the Cooperative and member districts as additional insureds or certificate holders;

(d) Be signed by an authorized representative of the insurance carrier;

(e) Be updated and resubmitted annually and upon any renewal or modification of coverage;

(f) Be provided to the Cooperative's business office at 800 South Plum Grove Road, Palatine, Illinois 60067.

6.8 Changes in Coverage and Notice. In the event that Contractor's insurance is cancelled, non-renewed, or materially modified in a manner that reduces coverage or changes terms, Contractor shall immediately notify the Cooperative in writing within two (2) business days. Failure to maintain required insurance, or failure to notify the Cooperative of changes in coverage, shall constitute a material breach of this Agreement and shall be grounds for immediate termination.

6.9 Insurance is Not Limiting. Contractor's maintenance of insurance shall not limit Contractor's indemnification obligations under Section 7 of this Agreement or Contractor's liability for damages arising from Contractor's negligence, breach, or violation of law.

7. INDEMNIFICATION

7.1 Indemnity by Contractor. Contractor shall defend (with counsel approved by the Cooperative), indemnify, and hold harmless the Cooperative, each of its member districts, their respective successors, and their respective officers, agents, employees, and representatives from and against any and all claims, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, judgments, settlements, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys' fees, court costs, and expert witness fees) arising out of or resulting from any of the following:

(a) Contractor's performance or failure to perform its obligations under this Agreement;

(b) Contractor's breach of any representation, warranty, or covenant contained in this Agreement;

(c) Bodily injury, death, or property damage to any person or property caused by Contractor or Contractor's employees, agents, or subcontractors;

(d) Contractor's failure to comply with applicable laws and regulations, including federal, state, and local laws, building codes, plumbing codes, mechanical codes, OSHA requirements, prevailing wage requirements, and anti-discrimination laws;

(e) Any liens, judgments, wage claims, or material supplier claims filed against any member district's property, accounts, or funds as a result of Contractor's failure to pay workers, suppliers, or subcontractors;

(f) Any acts, omissions, errors, or negligence of Contractor's employees, agents, or subcontractors;

(g) Injury to Contractor's own employees or third parties caused by Contractor's work;

(h) Property damage caused by Contractor's work, including damage to existing building systems, finishes, or contents.

7.2 Sole Negligence Exception. Contractor's indemnity obligation shall not extend to claims arising solely from the gross negligence or willful misconduct of the Cooperative or a member district, provided that:

(a) This exception shall not limit Contractor's indemnity for claims arising from the concurrent or comparative negligence of the Cooperative, member districts, and Contractor, in which case Contractor shall indemnify to the fullest extent permitted by applicable Illinois law;

(b) This exception shall not apply to claims for which Contractor is partially responsible or contributorily negligent.

7.3 Defense and Legal Representation. Contractor shall be responsible for and shall promptly pay all costs of defending any claim subject to indemnification under this Section 7, including costs of Contractor's counsel and all defense expenses. The Cooperative may select its own counsel to participate in defense, with costs shared as permitted by law. Contractor's defense counsel shall be subject to the Cooperative's reasonable approval, and approval shall not be unreasonably withheld.

7.4 Priority of Contractor's Insurance. Contractor's indemnity obligation shall apply independently of Contractor's insurance coverage and shall not be limited by insurance policy limits. Contractor's commercial general liability insurance and other coverage shall be primary with respect to claims arising from Contractor's work, and any insurance maintained by the Cooperative or member districts shall be excess.

7.5 Survival. Contractor's indemnification obligations shall survive termination or expiration of this Agreement with respect to claims arising from work performed during the contract term.

